1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,784
Sam had the clip open, ready to schedule.

2
00:00:02,784 --> 00:00:07,771
It looked finished: the avatar (an AI-generated on-screen presenter, standing in for

3
00:00:07,771 --> 00:00:11,512
an actor) delivered the line well, nothing suggested a problem.

4
00:00:11,512 --> 00:00:15,489
Then he scrolled the tool's pricing page out of habit and found it.

5
00:00:15,489 --> 00:00:19,289
The plan he'd generated on covered personal, organic posts only.

6
00:00:19,289 --> 00:00:24,039
Paid campaigns needed a separate commercial licence tier, meaning what your plan

7
00:00:24,039 --> 00:00:29,026
actually permits you to do with the footage, and this draft was never covered by it.

8
00:00:29,026 --> 00:00:30,451
He hadn't been careless.

9
00:00:30,451 --> 00:00:35,200
Everything up to now generated a product, a scene, a colour, nothing that needed

10
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,634
consent, because none of it was a person.

11
00:00:37,634 --> 00:00:42,206
An avatar is different: a real person licensed their likeness, or a synthetic

12
00:00:42,206 --> 00:00:47,193
performer stands in for one, and what you're allowed to do with it depends on which.

13
00:00:47,193 --> 00:00:49,805
So what actually changes about how you work?

14
00:00:49,805 --> 00:00:54,733
One habit, the exact one Sam had just skipped: check rights first, generate second,

15
00:00:54,733 --> 00:00:55,386
every time.

16
00:00:55,386 --> 00:00:57,998
That runs against how these tools are built.

17
00:00:57,998 --> 00:01:02,748
A generator renders a convincing clip whether or not the licence behind the face

18
00:01:02,748 --> 00:01:07,260
covers your plan, and a rendered clip gives no visual sign anything's wrong.

19
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:09,931
That's why it lands on you, not the platform.

20
00:01:09,931 --> 00:01:14,087
Meta and TikTok police their own policy, not your avatar tool's terms.

21
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:19,746
Here's the landscape as it actually stands, in three categories rather than a list

22
00:01:19,746 --> 00:01:23,110
of brand names that will be out of date within months.

23
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:28,094
A stock avatar is a real actor's likeness, licensed to whichever platform you're

24
00:01:28,094 --> 00:01:28,468
using.

25
00:01:28,468 --> 00:01:33,389
A custom avatar clones a specific real person, a founder or a real creator, and

26
00:01:33,389 --> 00:01:38,498
needs their documented consent, not just a general terms-of-service click-through.

27
00:01:38,498 --> 00:01:43,232
A synthetic avatar has no real person behind it at all, which is usually the

28
00:01:43,232 --> 00:01:48,154
simplest rights picture, though it still needs the same disclosure you'll build

29
00:01:48,154 --> 00:01:49,649
properly in Section 6.3.

30
00:01:49,649 --> 00:01:54,820
Notice all three still end in the same place: some form of disclosure, some form of

31
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:55,692
documentation.

32
00:01:55,692 --> 00:01:59,368
Nothing in this landscape lets you skip paperwork entirely.

33
00:01:59,368 --> 00:02:01,735
It only changes whose paperwork it is.

34
00:02:01,735 --> 00:02:06,906
Most people choosing between the three ask the wrong first question, which is which

35
00:02:06,906 --> 00:02:07,840
one looks best.

36
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:12,077
Ask instead which rights picture you're actually prepared to manage.

37
00:02:12,077 --> 00:02:16,251
A stock avatar means checking a licence tier before every campaign.

38
00:02:16,251 --> 00:02:21,048
A custom avatar means holding someone's documented consent and being ready to

39
00:02:21,048 --> 00:02:21,733
produce it.

40
00:02:21,733 --> 00:02:26,468
A synthetic avatar means the lightest paperwork of the three, but still real

41
00:02:26,468 --> 00:02:27,714
paperwork, not none.

42
00:02:27,714 --> 00:02:32,947
Pick based on which obligation you'll actually keep up with, not which face you like

43
00:02:32,947 --> 00:02:34,193
best in a demo reel.

44
00:02:34,743 --> 00:02:39,932
Here's what actually happened on Harlan's account, and it's a genuinely easy mistake

45
00:02:39,932 --> 00:02:41,661
to make, not a careless one.

46
00:02:41,661 --> 00:02:45,800
Sam generated a strong first draft on a library's entry-level plan.

47
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:50,865
The video looked right, the performance was convincing, and nothing about watching

48
00:02:50,865 --> 00:02:52,595
it back suggested a problem.

49
00:02:52,595 --> 00:02:57,784
The problem was in the terms: that tier licensed the avatar for personal and organic

50
00:02:57,784 --> 00:02:59,266
use, not paid campaigns.

51
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:04,146
Paid social needed a separate commercial tier, one draft one was never actually

52
00:03:04,146 --> 00:03:04,826
covered by.

53
00:03:04,826 --> 00:03:09,829
It got caught before any spend, and the fix was a recast on the right tier, not a

54
00:03:09,829 --> 00:03:12,238
different avatar or a different script.

55
00:03:12,238 --> 00:03:17,242
Notice what didn't need to change: the script, the avatar's appearance, the whole

56
00:03:17,242 --> 00:03:18,416
creative direction.

57
00:03:18,416 --> 00:03:23,419
One line item in a pricing page was the entire gap between a compliant ad and one

58
00:03:23,419 --> 00:03:24,160
that wasn't.

59
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:28,979
That's usually how this mistake looks in practice, small and easy to miss, not

60
00:03:28,979 --> 00:03:29,534
dramatic.

61
00:03:29,534 --> 00:03:34,662
Sam's habit now: before any avatar clip goes near an ad account, he screenshots the

62
00:03:34,662 --> 00:03:37,812
licence tier page and drops it in the brief folder.

63
00:03:37,812 --> 00:03:43,001
Not distrust of the tool, just a refusal to repeat "I checked once, months ago", the

64
00:03:43,001 --> 00:03:46,645
exact assumption that let draft one through the first time.

65
00:03:47,195 --> 00:03:52,324
I want to be precise about what's actually true here, checked at the time of writing

66
00:03:52,324 --> 00:03:53,546
rather than assumed.

67
00:03:53,546 --> 00:03:55,439
Tools genuinely differ on this.

68
00:03:55,439 --> 00:03:59,957
Some platforms ship commercial rights on every plan, entry-level included.

69
00:03:59,957 --> 00:04:04,964
Others reserve paid, commercial use for a higher tier, exactly the gap that caught

70
00:04:04,964 --> 00:04:06,246
Harlan's first draft.

71
00:04:06,246 --> 00:04:08,567
Neither approach is hidden or unusual.

72
00:04:08,567 --> 00:04:13,574
It's just a detail that's easy to miss once, expensive to miss on a live campaign,

73
00:04:13,574 --> 00:04:18,581
and worth checking fresh before every single campaign, not just the first time you

74
00:04:18,581 --> 00:04:19,985
signed up for the tool.

75
00:04:19,985 --> 00:04:22,061
Where do you actually go to check?

76
00:04:22,061 --> 00:04:27,068
The tool's own pricing or licence page, not a support article someone linked you a

77
00:04:27,068 --> 00:04:29,083
year ago, and not a forum thread.

78
00:04:29,083 --> 00:04:34,029
Pricing pages change more often than the tools themselves get rebuilt, and a tier

79
00:04:34,029 --> 00:04:39,036
that was commercial-inclusive when you signed up can be restructured later without

80
00:04:39,036 --> 00:04:41,418
your account automatically catching up.

81
00:04:41,418 --> 00:04:46,119
Five minutes on the actual current page beats an assumption carried over from

82
00:04:46,119 --> 00:04:47,768
whenever you first read it.

83
00:04:47,768 --> 00:04:50,882
This is also why the course is built the way it is.

84
00:04:50,882 --> 00:04:55,645
Naming one tool's exact terms here would be accurate for a few months and then

85
00:04:55,645 --> 00:05:00,225
quietly wrong, which is a worse outcome than teaching you the check itself.

86
00:05:00,225 --> 00:05:04,255
The check survives every pricing page rewrite a vendor ever ships.

87
00:05:04,805 --> 00:05:09,650
Licence tier isn't the only rights question, and it's worth naming the other one

88
00:05:09,650 --> 00:05:11,467
before you leave this lecture.

89
00:05:11,467 --> 00:05:16,374
If an avatar is meant to represent a real, specific person, a founder using their

90
00:05:16,374 --> 00:05:21,340
own likeness, or a real customer, that needs documented consent, not an assumption

91
00:05:21,340 --> 00:05:23,763
that it's fine because they agreed once.

92
00:05:23,763 --> 00:05:28,548
Some platforms now ask for that consent to be uploaded directly, with a name, a

93
00:05:28,548 --> 00:05:32,849
description of use, and a signature, not just kept in a filing cabinet.

94
00:05:32,849 --> 00:05:37,936
Both threads, the licence and the likeness, get their full treatment in Section six.

95
00:05:37,936 --> 00:05:42,903
Think about why platforms have tightened this specifically for likeness and voice,

96
00:05:42,903 --> 00:05:45,629
rather than for generated products or scenes.

97
00:05:45,629 --> 00:05:50,232
A generated scene can't be defamed and doesn't have a reputation to protect.

98
00:05:50,232 --> 00:05:54,896
A real person's face and voice, cloned convincingly enough to sell something,

99
00:05:54,896 --> 00:05:59,863
absolutely can be, which is exactly the harm this documentation requirement exists

100
00:05:59,863 --> 00:06:00,529
to prevent.

101
00:06:00,529 --> 00:06:02,649
It's not friction for its own sake.

102
00:06:02,649 --> 00:06:07,555
It's the platform asking the same question you should already be asking yourself:

103
00:06:07,555 --> 00:06:12,340
does this specific person actually know their likeness is selling this specific

104
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:12,825
product?

105
00:06:13,375 --> 00:06:18,298
Next lecture writes the actual script this avatar is going to perform, now that you

106
00:06:18,298 --> 00:06:21,323
know exactly what you're allowed to generate it on.

107
00:06:21,323 --> 00:06:26,128
Before you move on, do the one thing this whole lecture was building toward: open

108
00:06:26,128 --> 00:06:30,992
whichever avatar tool you're planning to use and confirm, today, which tier you're

109
00:06:30,992 --> 00:06:31,704
actually on.

110
00:06:31,704 --> 00:06:32,653
Not from memory.

111
00:06:32,653 --> 00:06:33,899
From the page itself.

112
00:06:33,899 --> 00:06:38,526
That single check is worth more than everything else in this lecture combined,

113
00:06:38,526 --> 00:06:43,508
because it's the one that would have stopped Harlan's first draft before it ever got

114
00:06:43,508 --> 00:06:44,102
generated.

115
00:06:44,102 --> 00:06:45,644
Screenshot it and keep it.

116
00:06:45,644 --> 00:06:50,211
It's the first line of the B04 brief you'll fill out three lectures from now.
